#{[17592186287959 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2x & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2x & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4-15)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-6x)\\mathbf{j}+(5x-4x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-11,4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121} & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+121 & =393 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =272 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 4.$"] [17592186287960 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((5)(0)-(3)(4))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(0)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(3)-(2)(5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-12)\\mathbf{i}-(0-8)\\mathbf{j}+(3-10)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287961 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((2)(-3)-(2)(0))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)(-3)-(0)(0))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)(2)-(0)(2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6-0)\\mathbf{j}+(-4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-6\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287972 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)-3(3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =4-3-16 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287973 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287974 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (2\\mathbf{b}\\times (-\\mathbf{c})) & =8 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =8 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287975 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{b}\\times 3\\mathbf{c}) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287976 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (6\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =90 \\\\ 3\\cdot 6\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =90 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290547 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =(1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186290552 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"] [17592186290553 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"] [17592186290554 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =(1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =1-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"] [17592186292832 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-18-0)\\mathbf{j}+(0-(-10))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+18\\mathbf{j}+10\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292833 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-2x-10x)\\mathbf{j}+(0-6x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186292837 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"] [17592186292839 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},10,-10⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =3+0+20 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292840 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292915 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -3 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3-2 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\le 3-2 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 5 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-5 \\\\ -5 & \\le -5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 5 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le 5.$"] [17592186295036 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(5\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(5\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8+3)\\mathbf{i}-(20-6)\\mathbf{j}+(5+4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =11\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-14\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+9\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295037 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x-2)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4-4x+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+24 & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$"] [17592186295221 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8-5)\\mathbf{i}-(6+2)\\mathbf{j}+(-15-8)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-8\\mathbf{j}-23\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295222 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295223 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{v