cending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186295237 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 3 & 0 \\\\ -x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x+0)\\mathbf{j}+(0+3x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-x,3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+9 & =49 \\\\ 10{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=2.$"] [17592186295238 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(3x-1)\\mathbf{j}+(0-1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,1-3x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(1-3x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11} & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-6x+11 & =10 \\\\ (3x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186295239 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-2)\\mathbf{j}+(0-1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2-2x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-2x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(4{x}^{2}-8x+4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (2x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$"] [17592186295240 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4-0)\\mathbf{i}-(x-5)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,5-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(5-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+(25-10x+{x}^{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57} & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+57 & =33 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+24 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,\\hspace{1em}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=4,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186295241 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\mathbf{i}-(4x-12)\\mathbf{j}+(0-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,12-4x,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(12-4x)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(144-96x+16{x}^{2})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157} & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x+157 & =157 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x & =0 \\\\ 16x(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\hspace{1em}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186299580 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨\\sqrt{7},-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨\\sqrt{7},3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{7})+(-2)(3)+(5)(-1) \\\\  & =7-6-5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299581 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{8},\\hspace{0.167em}4,\\hspace{0.167em}-3⟩\\cdot ⟨\\sqrt{8},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{8})(\\sqrt{8})+(4)(-1)+(-3)(2) \\\\  & =-8-4-6 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299582 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩\\cdot ⟨7,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩ \\\\  & =(0)(7)+(5)(-2)+(\\sqrt{11})(\\sqrt{11}) \\\\  & =0-10+11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299583 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 1)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(19)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(2)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(8) \\\\  & =38+2+24 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299584 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(1)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(22)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(-4) \\\\  & =1-44-12 \\\\  & =-55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299585 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =0(-2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(-6)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\hspace{0.167em}(-7) \\\\  & =0+12-35 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299586 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 6)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 6-0\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(12)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(14)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(24) \\\\  & =12-28+72 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299587 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(8)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(2)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(-25) \\\\  & =16-2-75 \\\\  & =-61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299588 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(5)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(15)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(2) \\\\  & =5+0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299589 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(-5)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(-20)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(-15) \\\\  & =-10+20+0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299590 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}((-1)(-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(7)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\hspace{0.167em}(-10)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(9) \\\\  & =-7-20+27 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299594 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-1,6⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =8-3-6 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299922 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302048 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"] [17592186302049 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}2t & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"] [17592186302103 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$"] [17592186302104 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"] [17592186302105 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.\n$$"] [17592186302106 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$"] [17592186302364 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-3 \\\\ -3 & \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le y\\le 3.$"] [17592186302365 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$"] [17592186302369 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4+1 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 4+1 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.\\