 parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335820 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335821 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1} \\\\  & \\phantom{=}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\ope